This is the base class for {@link Ext.grid.Grid} cells.

{@link Ext.grid.Row} create cells based on the {@link Ext.grid.column.Column#cfg!cell Ext.grid.column.Column#cell}
config. Application code would rarely create cells directly.

This is the default cell class for {@link Ext.grid.Grid} cells. Use this class if
you use the {@link Ext.grid.column.Column#cfg!renderer Ext.grid.column.Column#renderer} or {@link Ext.grid.column.Column#cfg!tpl Ext.grid.column.Column#tpl}
configs for a column.

{@link Ext.grid.Row} create cells based on the {@link Ext.grid.column.Column#cfg!cell Ext.grid.column.Column#cell}
config. Application code would rarely create cells directly.

A renderer is a method which can be used to transform data (value, appearance, etc.)
before it is rendered.

For example:

     {
         text: 'Some column',
         dataIndex: 'fieldName',

         renderer: function (value, record) {
             if (value === 1) {
                 return '1 person';
             }
             return value + ' people';
         }
     }

If a string is supplied, it should be the name of a renderer method from the
appropriate {@link Ext.app.ViewController}.

This config is only processed if the {@link #cell} type is the default of
{@link Ext.grid.cell.Cell}.

**Note** See {@link Ext.grid.Grid} documentation for other, better alternatives
to rendering cell content.

An {@link Ext.XTemplate}, or an XTemplate *definition string* to use
to process a {@link Ext.data.Model} data to produce a cell's rendered
value.

    @example
